1
00:00:00,910 --> 00:00:02,990
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:03,650
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,050 --> 00:00:07,550
My name is Catherine and today we're talking a little bit about small talk.

4
00:00:07,790 --> 00:00:08,190
That's right.

5
00:00:08,430 --> 00:00:10,350
We are getting towards the end of our series.

6
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:25,330
Actually this is the last podcast that we will have all about small talk and we're just going to be following Mr. Campbell and Michelle and Melissa and they're just going to talk very briefly about the weather, about traveling, very casual things.

7
00:00:25,710 --> 00:00:26,110
That's right.

8
00:00:26,330 --> 00:00:30,110
So let's take a listen to this dialogue and we'll be back in a moment to discuss what's going on.

9
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:34,620
Hi ladies.

10
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:42,200
Thanks for picking me up.

11
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:43,250
It's awful weather out there.

12
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:45,180
Absolutely.

13
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:47,020
It's been raining for hours.

14
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:48,440
How are you Melissa?

15
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:49,300
Are you okay?

16
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:51,180
I'm great thanks Mr. Campbell.

17
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:54,080
Do you have any business trips planned soon, Mr. Campbell?

18
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:54,940
Of course.

19
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:55,760
I'm always traveling.

20
00:00:56,700 --> 00:00:59,920
I'm leaving for London next Monday and then I'll fly to Boston from there.

21
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:00,880
It's going to be a busy month.

22
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:02,100
How about you Michelle?

23
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:04,260
Do you have any vacation plans?

24
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:05,140
Yes.

25
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:11,280
Mike and I will travel to Beijing to see Mike's parents for the Spring Festival, and hopefully next year we will visit London.

26
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:12,960
I hear it's a wonderful city.

27
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:14,280
I couldn't agree more.

28
00:01:14,620 --> 00:01:15,820
London is really fantastic.

29
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:17,120
It's my favorite city.

30
00:01:17,460 --> 00:01:18,500
I'm sure you'll have a great time.

31
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:23,240
All right.

32
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:24,240
We're back.

33
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:25,720
So now let's take a look at language takeaway.

34
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:31,480
All right.

35
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:35,420
We don't have that much today in language takeaway but we do have a couple of really important words and phrases.

36
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,840
The first one comes from the very first line.

37
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:39,560
Mr. Campbell says, hi ladies.

38
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:41,520
Thanks for picking me up.

39
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:42,120
That's right.

40
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:47,500
Now this phrasal verb, to pick someone up, means to get them, right?

41
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:49,820
Yeah, to gather them, to collect them.

42
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:54,020
You can do this with a car like I will pick you up at the airport.

43
00:01:54,480 --> 00:02:00,400
But you can also do it in person by just walking to a place and meeting somebody and then taking them somewhere else.

44
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:01,680
That's the important part.

45
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:02,700
You take them somewhere else.

46
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:08,840
So maybe if you go pick your kids up from school, you just walk there, you get them and you walk back home.

47
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:08,980
That's right.

48
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:16,160
And so the key here is that you remember that the verb goes first, I will pick and then you have the person.

49
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:16,990
I will pick you up.

50
00:02:17,210 --> 00:02:18,330
I will pick them up.

51
00:02:18,610 --> 00:02:20,050
He will pick us up.

52
00:02:20,310 --> 00:02:20,690
That's right.

53
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:23,790
So the subject goes between pick and up.

54
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:24,350
That's right.

55
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:26,200
All right.

56
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:27,200
So we're going to pick someone up.

57
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:29,310
And then Mr. Campbell comments on the weather.

58
00:02:29,510 --> 00:02:32,050
He says it's awful weather out there.

59
00:02:32,430 --> 00:02:33,030
It's awful.

60
00:02:33,690 --> 00:02:33,930
All right.

61
00:02:33,930 --> 00:02:38,890
So awful, you listen to the tone, you automatically think that this is probably a bad thing.

62
00:02:39,390 --> 00:02:40,550
And you're right, it is a bad thing.

63
00:02:40,790 --> 00:02:42,330
But what's another way to say awful?

64
00:02:43,010 --> 00:02:43,490
It's terrible.

65
00:02:44,050 --> 00:02:44,530
It's bad.

66
00:02:44,930 --> 00:02:45,470
It's horrible.

67
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:49,300
So awful is a way to say something's really bad.

68
00:02:49,640 --> 00:02:51,460
And we use it very often with the weather.

69
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:52,460
This weather is awful.

70
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:55,960
But we can also use it about other things like food, for example.

71
00:02:56,900 --> 00:03:01,360
So if you have something to eat and it doesn't taste good, you can say this is awful.

72
00:03:01,660 --> 00:03:02,020
What is this?

73
00:03:02,650 --> 00:03:04,590
Or what's that awful smell?

74
00:03:05,470 --> 00:03:07,350
Oh, Marco took his shoes off.

75
00:03:08,930 --> 00:03:10,250
Right, an awful smell.

76
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:12,080
All right.

77
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:18,050
And well, Mr. Campbell went on to tell them that he's taking another trip soon, but a specific kind of trip.

78
00:03:18,530 --> 00:03:18,770
All right.

79
00:03:18,970 --> 00:03:23,350
So you know that there are many different kinds of trips, but the one that he's taking is a business trip.

80
00:03:23,970 --> 00:03:27,210
So normally we could just say I'm going on a trip next year.

81
00:03:27,850 --> 00:03:30,150
But here we specify, we say it's a business trip.

82
00:03:30,370 --> 00:03:32,510
So what are we doing on this trip, Marco?

83
00:03:32,690 --> 00:03:32,990
That's right.

84
00:03:33,190 --> 00:03:34,350
So we're basically working.

85
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:39,400
We're going because our company needs us to go to a meeting or to do some work abroad.

86
00:03:40,170 --> 00:03:40,400
All right.

87
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:50,240
So you know on a business trip that you'll be doing work things, you'll be having meetings, you will not be going to Disney World, you will not be meeting with friends and going to movies probably.

88
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:50,870
Right, exactly.

89
00:03:51,330 --> 00:03:53,010
So it's very different from a vacation.

90
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:57,190
A vacation you go with the objective of going to these fun places.

91
00:03:57,760 --> 00:03:59,750
So can I say I'm going on a business vacation?

92
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:03,770
No, you're either going on vacation or you're taking a business trip.

93
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:05,880
All right, business trip.

94
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:08,090
So now let's take a break and let's review our dialogue.

95
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:13,400
Hi, ladies.

96
00:04:19,270 --> 00:04:20,130
Thanks for picking me up.

97
00:04:20,370 --> 00:04:21,490
It's awful weather out there.

98
00:04:22,990 --> 00:04:23,410
Absolutely.

99
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:25,290
It's been raining for hours.

100
00:04:26,050 --> 00:04:26,650
How are you, Melissa?

101
00:04:27,130 --> 00:04:27,530
Are you okay?

102
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:30,120
I'm great, thanks, Mr. Campbell.

103
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:32,310
Do you have any business trips planned soon, Mr. Campbell?

104
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:33,970
Of course, I'm always traveling.

105
00:04:34,570 --> 00:04:37,530
I'll leave for London next Monday and then I'll fly to Boston from there.

106
00:04:38,030 --> 00:04:39,090
It's going to be a busy month.

107
00:04:39,690 --> 00:04:40,310
How about you, Michelle?

108
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:42,440
Do you have any vacation plans?

109
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:49,480
Yes, Mike and I will travel to Beijing to see Mike's parents for the spring festival and hopefully next year we will visit London.

110
00:04:49,980 --> 00:04:51,160
I hear it's a wonderful city.

111
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:52,480
I couldn't agree more.

112
00:04:52,800 --> 00:04:54,060
London is really fantastic.

113
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:55,600
It's my favorite city.

114
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:56,700
I'm sure you'll have a great time.

115
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:06,920
All right, so now let's take a look at four key phrases on Fluency Builder.

116
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:07,200
Fluency Builder.

117
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:12,040
All right, now we have a great phrase for everybody here in Fluency Builder.

118
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,900
Melissa is talking to Mr. Campbell and he asks her how she is.

119
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:16,760
Are you okay?

120
00:05:16,860 --> 00:05:18,760
And she says, I'm great, thanks.

121
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:23,100
Now Marco, why would we use this phrase instead of I'm fine, thank you?

122
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:24,880
Well, it's basically the same thing.

123
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:30,040
It's just another way of answering this common question, how are you?

124
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:31,600
You can say I'm fine, thank you.

125
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:33,480
I'm well.

126
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:36,570
But this is just another way of saying I'm okay.

127
00:05:37,010 --> 00:05:44,130
All right, but I think it's important too to remember that this is also very casual like the rest of the conversation and pretty enthusiastic.

128
00:05:44,840 --> 00:05:45,250
She's great.

129
00:05:46,030 --> 00:05:53,910
So, it's important to remember that I'm fine, thanks is standard, it's very normal, but I'm great is a nice way to change it and do something different.

130
00:05:54,150 --> 00:05:54,630
That's right.

131
00:05:54,870 --> 00:05:55,010
Okay.

132
00:05:55,570 --> 00:06:01,610
And now we wanted to ask if we have any plans, but we wanted to know if we have any vacation plans.

133
00:06:02,190 --> 00:06:07,090
And the interesting thing about this question was that Mr. Campbell said, any vacation plans?

134
00:06:07,550 --> 00:06:08,870
So that's the whole sentence.

135
00:06:09,330 --> 00:06:11,350
Well, first he says, how about you, Michelle?

136
00:06:12,090 --> 00:06:12,930
Any vacation plans?

137
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:15,960
So what we think here is that maybe it's wrong.

138
00:06:15,960 --> 00:06:19,510
Do you have any vacation plans is something that we'd want to hear, right?

139
00:06:19,670 --> 00:06:19,830
Right.

140
00:06:20,220 --> 00:06:24,320
But here we don't actually need to say, do you have any vacation plans?

141
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:26,960
Because it's pretty clear who we're talking to.

142
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:28,260
We're talking to Michelle, right?

143
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:29,000
Right.

144
00:06:29,440 --> 00:06:32,080
And we're talking about traveling and we're talking about plans.

145
00:06:32,840 --> 00:06:38,310
So it's overstated that we don't really need to say, do you have any vacation plans?

146
00:06:38,510 --> 00:06:40,190
We're already talking about the subject.

147
00:06:40,590 --> 00:06:42,410
Now, this is a great way to transition.

148
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:48,120
One person is talking about himself or herself and then you want to talk about the other person.

149
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:49,330
So you say, what about you?

150
00:06:50,760 --> 00:06:55,200
Any vacation plans or any dinner plans or any ideas?

151
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:56,200
Exactly.

152
00:06:56,200 --> 00:07:01,730
So it's a great way to, like you say, transition from one idea or from one person to another.

153
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:10,110
Now to the next phrase, a very common one, and specifically when you are not really sure about something, but somebody told you.

154
00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:15,830
Specifically in this case, Michelle says, I hear it's a wonderful city when talking about London.

155
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:18,750
So what does she mean by I hear it's a wonderful city?

156
00:07:19,030 --> 00:07:29,570
Well basically Michelle is saying she's never been to London, but she's read or she's heard from friends or from a TV show that London's a wonderful city.

157
00:07:29,730 --> 00:07:39,270
So she can't say it is a wonderful city because she's never been there, but she can say, listen, you know, I have, I have heard this from other people or from magazines.

158
00:07:39,970 --> 00:07:42,830
So it's not even clear where she heard it, but that's not really important.

159
00:07:43,250 --> 00:07:53,650
So basically the idea is that we don't know personally, but we have friends that told us or we have read in a magazine or a TV show, we've seen it, that it's great.

160
00:07:53,930 --> 00:07:55,750
So you can use it with many things like a restaurant.

161
00:07:56,340 --> 00:07:56,840
That's right.

162
00:07:57,720 --> 00:07:58,720
I hear the food is delicious.

163
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:02,040
Or I could say, hey Marco, I'm going to Hong Kong Disneyland next year.

164
00:08:02,580 --> 00:08:02,860
Right.

165
00:08:02,980 --> 00:08:05,580
And I can say, oh, I hear that it's a very fun place.

166
00:08:05,920 --> 00:08:07,040
So Marco, you've never been there, right?

167
00:08:07,160 --> 00:08:07,940
No, I've never been there.

168
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:09,160
Okay.

169
00:08:09,160 --> 00:08:09,460
All right.

170
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:15,280
And to finish off, Mr. Campbell agrees completely about London being such a wonderful city.

171
00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:18,140
And he says, I couldn't agree more.

172
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:20,120
I couldn't agree more.

173
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:21,780
So he agrees.

174
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:23,980
We understand that.

175
00:08:23,980 --> 00:08:28,240
But I couldn't agree more is like saying, I really, really agree.

176
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:28,760
Right.

177
00:08:29,160 --> 00:08:30,680
Like you are completely right.

178
00:08:31,320 --> 00:08:31,540
Yeah.

179
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:34,520
So this is a way to exaggerate.

180
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:35,460
You know, you want to say, I agree.

181
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:36,520
Eh, it's a little boring.

182
00:08:36,900 --> 00:08:39,280
So you say, I couldn't agree more.

183
00:08:39,460 --> 00:08:41,160
I'm agreeing the most I can.

184
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:42,000
That's right.

185
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:47,200
And then we can also use the opposite, which would be if you disagree with someone.

186
00:08:47,880 --> 00:08:50,040
I couldn't disagree more.

187
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:51,840
So you just change that one word.

188
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:53,520
I couldn't disagree more.

189
00:08:53,520 --> 00:08:54,500
So maybe I don't like London.

190
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:57,240
Maybe it's too rainy or too dark for me.

191
00:08:57,440 --> 00:08:59,080
So I say, I couldn't disagree more.

192
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:00,080
I think London is terrible.

193
00:09:01,640 --> 00:09:06,480
Maybe not the right thing to say in a small talk situation, but we can talk about that in a minute.

194
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:07,480
Exactly.

195
00:09:07,480 --> 00:09:08,480
All right.

196
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:10,480
So let's review everything one last time.

197
00:09:18,760 --> 00:09:19,320
Hi, ladies.

198
00:09:19,720 --> 00:09:20,460
Thanks for picking me up.

199
00:09:20,720 --> 00:09:21,800
It's awful weather out there.

200
00:09:23,310 --> 00:09:23,750
Absolutely.

201
00:09:24,510 --> 00:09:25,630
It's been raining for hours.

202
00:09:26,410 --> 00:09:26,990
How are you, Melissa?

203
00:09:27,470 --> 00:09:27,850
Are you okay?

204
00:09:28,390 --> 00:09:28,770
I'm great.

205
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:30,560
Thanks, Mr. Campbell.

206
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:32,650
Do you have any business trips planned soon, Mr. Campbell?

207
00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:33,510
Of course.

208
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:35,240
I'm always traveling.

209
00:09:35,240 --> 00:09:38,160
I'm leaving for London next Monday, and then I'll fly to Boston from there.

210
00:09:38,370 --> 00:09:39,430
It's going to be a busy month.

211
00:09:39,970 --> 00:09:40,630
How about you, Michelle?

212
00:09:41,330 --> 00:09:42,810
Do you have any vacation plans?

213
00:09:43,330 --> 00:09:43,690
Yes.

214
00:09:44,210 --> 00:09:49,830
Mike and I will travel to Beijing to see Mike's parents for the spring festival, and hopefully next year we will visit London.

215
00:09:50,250 --> 00:09:51,510
I hear it's a wonderful city.

216
00:09:51,850 --> 00:09:52,830
I couldn't agree more.

217
00:09:53,150 --> 00:09:54,370
London is really fantastic.

218
00:09:54,910 --> 00:09:55,670
It's my favorite city.

219
00:09:56,010 --> 00:09:57,030
I'm sure you'll have a great time.

220
00:10:00,370 --> 00:10:00,750
All right.

221
00:10:00,920 --> 00:10:03,290
So this is our last podcast all about small talk.

222
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:05,170
And I hope it's been useful.

223
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:14,950
We've gotten some great feedback about the different topics and small conversations you can have with a stranger, a co-worker, a friend, to kind of not have that awkward silence.

224
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:16,070
That's right.

225
00:10:17,830 --> 00:10:18,930
We've gone over a lot of different situations.

226
00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:23,310
We've had situations with strangers, situations with work friends.

227
00:10:24,110 --> 00:10:28,230
But I think what's really important is that in all of these situations, we're very polite.

228
00:10:28,610 --> 00:10:29,490
We say nice things.

229
00:10:30,210 --> 00:10:35,210
There are these transitions like I hear or I couldn't agree more.

230
00:10:35,430 --> 00:10:39,590
These are wonderful ways of really being nice to the person you're talking to.

231
